From d9de7236dd65925532e179de800514f76745ec49 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Repo Audit <audit@local>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 07:53:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add benchmark infrastructure and CI regression gates (no
 production code changes)

- tests_correctness/property_test.py: reproducible 400,000-check property test
  (20,000 trials x 20 steps) validating PPMRuntime.process() against the
  documented delta/threshold/confidence contract.
- tests_correctness/differential_test.py + harness_c/: reproducible 7,500-step
  differential test proving Src/Ppm_edge.c and src/ppm_edge/runtime.py agree
  bit-for-bit given identical inputs.
- bench/bench_python.py: core operation latency (p50/p95/p99/p99.9), warm vs.
  cold-start latency, peak RSS memory, and tracemalloc-based allocation
  behavior for the Python implementation.
- bench/bench_c.c: core operation latency percentiles for the C implementation,
  plus a static-analysis confirmation of zero heap allocations in ppm_process.
- bench/run_benchmarks.sh: single command that captures the exact environment
  (git SHA, Python/gcc/glibc versions, OS, CPU), runs all correctness tests,
  and runs both benchmark suites, writing everything to a timestamped results
  directory.
- bench/thresholds.json + bench/check_regression_thresholds.py: CI regression
  gate. Correctness thresholds are zero-tolerance. Performance thresholds are
  set with a large safety margin over the measured local baseline to avoid
  flakiness on shared CI runners, while still catching real regressions
  (verified against an injected fake regression during development, see PR
  description).
- .github/workflows/Ci.yml: new correctness-and-benchmarks job that runs the
  above on every push/PR and uploads results as a build artifact.

No changes to Src/Ppm_edge.c, Src/Ppm_edge.h, src/ppm_edge/runtime.py, or any
other production code. This commit only adds measurement and CI
infrastructure, per audit request: measure first, optimize later, and only
if a benchmark identifies an actual bottleneck.
